  "period_note": "Year-end (31 December 2014) position from Midland Bank's own audited annual report \"MDB Financial Performance and Ratios: 5 Years Overview\", taken from the FY2015 report. SOLO / STANDALONE basis. Midland Bank was licensed in 2013, so FY2013 is the bank's first year of operation and this series covers its entire public history; there is no FY2011 or FY2012 to report. All figures are as reported by the bank; the source table prints total liabilities directly, so nothing on the balance sheet is derived. The row mapped to operating_profit is the bank's 'Profit before provision and tax' (labelled 'Profit before provision on loans and corporate tax' in some years), i.e. pre-provision operating profit. The FY2014 annual report extracts with no usable summary table (it is a scan or its embedded font defeats text extraction), so this year comes from the FY2015 report's comparative column. The NPL ratio is recorded as 0.00%: the FY2015, FY2016 and FY2017 reports all print a dash in the credit-quality rows for FY2013 and FY2014, which for a bank in its first two years of operation means nil rather than unknown. Midland Bank's first reported classified loans appear in FY2015 (140m, 1.01%). RESTATEMENTS -- 4 figure(s) here are reported differently by other Midland Bank annual reports; the contemporaneous value is kept and the divergence recorded: commission_exchange_brokerage: this record uses 40.37 (AR2015); AR2016 report 40; other_operating_income: this record uses 18.71 (AR2015); AR2016 report 19; tax_expense: this record uses 66.52 (AR2015); AR2016 report 67; profit_after_tax: this record uses 34.67 (AR2015); AR2016 report 35. Classified loans are calculated as the reported NPL percentage applied to reported loans and advances, because the source table prints the ratio but not the absolute figure for most years. The provision charge for the year is calculated as profit before provision and tax minus profit after provision before tax; the source table's own provision row wraps across two lines and its label is lost in text extraction in several reports. The derivation reproduces the printed figure exactly where it is readable (FY2025: 2,702 - 1,110 = 1,592; FY2024: 2,114 - 1,125 = 989; FY2023: 1,743 - 1,277 = 466).",
